# READ(2)

## NAME

read - read from file

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <unistd.h>

int read(int fildes, char *buffer, int nbytes);
```

## DESCRIPTION

A file descriptor is a word returned from a successful *open, creat, dup,* or *pipe* call.
*Buffer* is the location of *nbytes* contiguous bytes into which the input will be placed. It
is not guaranteed that all *nbytes* bytes will be read; for example if the file refers to a
typewriter at most one line will be returned. In any event the number of characters read is
returned.

If the returned value is 0, then end-of-file has been reached.

**Note: a directory can be read with an ordinary read here,** as it could in v7, and this
kernel allows it. That is what ttyname(3) does. It also means such a program works under the
kernel and *not* under b6sim, whose *read* is the host's and refuses a directory.

## SEE ALSO

open(2), creat(2), dup(2), pipe(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

As mentioned, 0 is returned when the end of the file has been reached. If the read was
otherwise unsuccessful the return value is -1. Many conditions can generate an error: physical
I/O errors, bad buffer address, preposterous *nbytes,* file descriptor not that of an input
file.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**nbytes is a byte count, not a word count,** and one word is *six* bytes. File I/O counts
bytes throughout, and a disk block is BSIZE == 3072 bytes == 512 words - which is what BUFSIZ
in <stdio.h> is set from, and why a page that says 512 bytes is describing a PDP-11.

*Buffer* is a fat **char \*** and the kernel decodes it as one, so an unaligned buffer is not
an error.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_read** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
